second artist, birth date for a different person, or second sport is evidence to retain it. Check each proposed deletion once more against all originals before producing the final short list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"What is the profession of John H. Williams?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 11, \"text\": \"with a B.S.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 12, \"end\": 27, \"text\": \"degree in 1928.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 28, \"end\": 136, \"text\": \"During his last year at the University, he was married to Vera Martin; the couple would have three children.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 137, \"end\": 248, \"text\": \"Williams performed his graduate studies at the University of California, Berkeley, where he was awarded an M.A.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 249, \"end\": 275, \"text\": \"degree in 1930 and a Ph.D.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 276, \"end\": 295, \"text\": \"the following year.\"}, \"D1S7\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 296, \"end\": 404, \"text\": \"In his first year of graduate school he published his first paper about a double crystal x-ray spectrometer.\"}, \"D1S8\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 405, \"end\": 593, \"text\": \"He joined the University of Chicago with a postdoctoral fellowship from the National Research Council during 1931–1933, then became an instructor of physics at the University of Minnesota.\"}, \"D1S9\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d00\", \"start\": 594, \"end\": 604, \"text\": \"In 1934 he\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 11, \"text\": \"With a B.S.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 12, \"end\": 27, \"text\": \"degree in 1928.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 28, \"end\": 136, \"text\": \"During his last year at the University, he was married to Vera Martin; the couple would have three children.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 137, \"end\": 248, \"text\": \"Williams performed his graduate studies at the University of California, Berkeley, where he was awarded an M.A.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 249, \"end\": 275, \"text\": \"degree in 1930 and a Ph.D.\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 276, \"end\": 295, \"text\": \"the following year.\"}, \"D2S7\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 296, \"end\": 405, \"text\": \"In his first year of graduate school, he published his first paper about a double crystal x-ray spectrometer.\"}, \"D2S8\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 406, \"end\": 602, \"text\": \"He joined the University of Chicago with a postdoctoral fellowship from the National Research Council during 1931–1933, then became a professional basketball player at the University of Minnesota.\"}, \"D2S9\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"start\": 603, \"end\": 698, \"text\": \"In 1934 he demonstrated exceptional skills on the court, leading his team to several victories.\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 141, \"text\": \"John Hartley Williams John Hartley Williams (7 February 1942 – 3 May 2014) was a British poet who was born in Cheshire and grew up in London.\"}, \"D3S2\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d02\", \"start\": 142, \"end\": 223, \"text\": \"He studied at the University of Nottingham and later at the University of London.\"}, \"D3S3\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d02\", \"start\": 224, \"end\": 274, \"text\": \"His poetry book \\\"Blues\\\" was shortlisted for the T.\"}, \"D3S4\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d02\", \"start\": 275, \"end\": 277, \"text\": \"S.\"}, \"D3S5\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d02\", \"start\": 278, \"end\": 290, \"text\": \"Eliot Prize.\"}, \"D3S6\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d02\", \"start\": 291, \"end\": 440, \"text\": \"He was a judge of the 2007 Poetry on the Lake poetry competition, a judge of the Keats-Shelley Prize for Poetry, and a tutor at the Arvon Foundation.\"}, \"D3S7\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d02\", \"start\": 441, \"end\": 495, \"text\": \"He died from cancer at his home in Berlin in May 2014.\"}, \"D3S8\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d02\", \"start\": 496, \"end\": 532, \"text\": \"He was survived by Gizella, his wife\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0140-7c917f2b27a5:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"John H. Williams\", \"scope\": \"1931–1933 and 1934\", \"claim\": \"John H. Williams became a professional basketball player at the University of Minnesota after his postdoctoral fellowship.\", \"answer\": \"professional basketball player\", \"quote\": \"He joined the University of Chicago with a postdoctoral fellowship from the National Research Council during 1931–1933, then became a pr